---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// On demand so the Accept-negotiation middleware runs (see src/middleware.ts).
export const prerender = false;

// Bump when the practices below change, not on every deploy.
const updated = '23 August 2026';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy — Kartik Labhshetwar"
  description="What this site collects, who processes it, and how to opt out. No ads, no tracking profiles, no data sold."
>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    This is a personal site. It has no accounts, no ads, and nothing to sell you. Below is everything it
    collects and every third party that sees any of it. Last updated {updated}.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground">Newsletter</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    If you subscribe, your email address is sent to Kit (formerly ConvertKit), which stores it and delivers
    the emails. Kit sends a confirmation email first, so nothing arrives unless you click the link in it.
    That address is used for one thing: telling you when there is a new post. It is never sold, rented, or
    shared, and every email carries a one-click unsubscribe link that removes you immediately.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground">Visitor count</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    The number in the footer is a count of unique visitors. Your browser computes an opaque identifier
    (via FingerprintJS) and the site stores that identifier in a set in Upstash Redis, purely so the same
    person is not counted twice across networks. If that fails, for example with JavaScript off or a blocker
    on, the server falls back to a non-reversible hash of your IP address and user agent instead. Either way
    what is stored is one opaque string in a set. No raw IP address is kept, nothing is tied to a name, and
    the only thing ever read back out is the total.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground">Analytics</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    Page analytics run through Databuddy, loaded from cdn.databuddy.cc. It records page views, outbound link
    clicks, interactions, and web vitals so I can see which posts get read and what is slow. Blocking that
    script, or using a browser that blocks it for you, does not break anything on this site.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground">Hosting and storage on your device</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    The site runs on Cloudflare Workers, so Cloudflare handles every request and keeps its own standard
    request logs. Your light or dark theme choice is stored in your browser's local storage and never leaves
    your device. GitHub's API is called from the server for star counts and the sponsor list; those requests
    carry nothing about you.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground">Your choices</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/75">
    Unsubscribe from any email to have your address removed. Block the analytics script or browse privately
    to stay out of the counts. If you want to know what is held about you or want it deleted, ask via
    <a href="/contact" class="underline underline-offset-4">the contact page</a> and I will handle it
    myself. This policy changes only when the practices above do, and the date at the top moves with it.
  </p>
</Base>
